; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What every rgbs variant shares, stated once: the first column of a suite
; that was five, then nine, and is now eight variants of one.
;
; WHAT THIS IS. Peeling a bonded sheet off what it is stuck to, cutting a
; sealed carton open and emptying it, feeding a cable through a closed bore,
; polishing marks off a strip against a spinning bit, and wiping stains off a
; bar with a foam sponge. Five jobs a person does at a bench with two hands,
; which shipped as five task suites and are now eight documents beside this
; file.
;
; WHY THEY MERGE. They already shared everything a benchmark cares about and
; agreed about none of it. All five stood two YAM arms behind one table and
; worked a small patch of it; all five wanted an overhead view and a close
; one. But each declared its own table, aimed its own cameras and named its
; own rig -- so a policy evaluated across the five saw a different observation
; space for every task it was asked to do, and none of the differences meant
; anything. This file is what that set of facts actually is.
;
; WHAT MERGING CHANGED, and it is two things. ONE TABLE: every variant stands
; in bench_075. The four peel variants stood in bench_0728 -- the same table
; 22 mm lower -- and moved; their layouts are all stated in the cell's
; work-surface frame, so the arithmetic transplanted untouched. ONE RIG:
; rgbs_views@v1 (staging.py) is the suite's only camera rig, three views at
; one resolution, one pair of fields of view and one set of channels,
; differing per variant only in where they are aimed. The geometry is
; polish's because it is the only one that was calibrated against a real
; overhead camera; a variant whose work sits elsewhere stages the same rig
; aimed at its own layout, as a constant of the module that owns the layout.
;
; WHAT MERGING DID NOT CHANGE: the physics. Five arrangements arrive under the
; names they were measured at, because a film integrated by VBD at 1/1200 s,
; a foam sponge on a foreign engine and a rod under a coupled proxy are not
; interchangeable and nothing about sharing a table says they are. So
; (:physics ...) is NOT here; every document states its own.
;
; AND ONE THING CHANGED THAT THE MERGE DID NOT REQUIRE. The peel variants also
; changed robot, from yam_bimanual to the yam_i2rt the other five ran on, so
; that the suite is one robot as well as one bench. That is a re-authoring
; rather than a port -- a different hand, different collision geometry,
; different finger servos -- and what it cost is recorded on
; peel/layout.py and in graphs/peel_gt/README.md rather than glossed
; here.
;
; A variant beside this file inherits every clause below: its own :staging
; clause wins KEY BY KEY (the three peel documents restate their embodiment,
; because they work with the left arm and no support arm), its :objects and
; :fixtures are its own, and its :artifacts merge by kind. No :fixtures are
; shared: three families each stand a work mat somewhere different, and a
; record that builds a thing builds it in one place.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (suite rgbs)

  (:language "Eight bimanual bench jobs behind one calibrated camera rig: peel an
              adhesive strip off a box or a protective film off a panel, cut a
              sealed carton open and empty it, feed a cable through a closed
              conduit, polish marks off a strip against a fixed spinning bit,
              and wipe stains off a bar with a foam sponge (YAM i2rt bimanual).")

  (:staging
    ; The one cell every variant stands in. It was bench_0728 for the peel
    ; variants; see the header.
    (:scene bench_075)
    ; The RIGHT hand works and the LEFT hand holds, on five of the eight, and
    ; the two arms are SOLID to each other on all eight. Each family measured
    ; why: polish crosses both hands over one strip repeatedly and measured
    ; link4 into link4 at 70-77 N; decant pins a carton with one hand while
    ; the other draws a blade 80 mm from it for the length of the seam; prep
    ; holds a 200 mm bar in the air with one hand while the other works along
    ; it, so the two corridors overlap for the whole episode; tunnel feeds
    ; with one hand while the other steadies the conduit; and in the peel
    ; variants only one arm peels, but the other stands over the same box
    ; while it does, and an idle arm is exactly the one nothing was checking.
    ; The planners must keep each other's corridors clear. See
    ; EmbodimentSpec.cross_arm_collisions.
    ;
    ; A staging key is overridden as one clause, so the peel documents restate
    ; the whole pairing -- (:work-arm left) and (:arms-solid true) together.
    (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
    ; The one rig, registered aimed at polish's drum contact -- the aim the
    ; calibration was transplanted onto, so `strip` stages it as is. Every
    ; other family aims it at its own work: (:cameras assets.<family>.layout:<NAME>_VIEWS).
    (:cameras rgbs_views@v1)
    (:control-mode joint_position)
    (:episodes (:count 1) (:seed 0) (:sampler fixed))
    ; Two arms: one works and one holds, and a one-armed robot is refused at
    ; build rather than discovered mid-rollout. The three peel documents say
    ; (:arms 1) for themselves -- one arm peels, and their hosted pairings
    ; include one-armed robots.
    (:arms 2)
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; How the bench looks under the path tracer: the real cell's gridded foam
  ; mats, its oak ledge and slat wall, and the lab lighting. Every variant
  ; renders under it; no variant's tools are here, because the three
  ; registrars are kept apart on purpose (tools.py says why) and three of the
  ; eight ship none.
  (:artifacts
    (:render "look:RGBS_LOOK")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:source "Five suites merged onto one cell and one camera rig. peel: adhesion
              after AutoPeel (ICRA 2025) restated in SI from the reference
              tape_render/tape_traj implementation, with the film's constants
              derived from published 60 um LDPE properties; box_decant, polish
              and surface_prep authored for this corpus from reference video and
              photographs of the real rig, each scoring an irreversible per-cell
              surface field -- Preston's law fed by the solver's own contact
              force, except surface_prep's, which is fed by the co-simulation
              bridge because a foreign engine's foam has no geom in MuJoCo's
              contact table; tunnel_route authored beside cable_route, whose rod
              mechanism and coupled-proxy arrangement it inherits. The overhead
              camera every variant now shares is polish's, solved 2026-08-04
              against a calibration board on the real bench. Physics regimes
              were probed before authoring: see
              scripts/{polish,prep,decant,tunnel}_bench.py and the
              tests/test_rgbs*.py modules.")
  )

  ; The order is curatorial and it is the order the five suites were authored
  ; in, which is also roughly the order of how much of the bench each one
  ; uses. A bare `rgbs` resolves to the first, so what leads matters:
  ; `handle` does. It led the peel family from the start on the strength of
  ; the manipulation it can be asked for -- the pull-tab is pinned rigid at
  ; box-top height, every accuracy-critical landmark solves under a tenth of a
  ; millimetre, and the shipped graph peels the whole strip. It carries the
  ; adhesion constants too: bond stiffness, break distance and areal density
  ; are the tape's own and not a layout's.
  ;
  ; `along_x` led this list until it was withdrawn. It was the layout the
  ; adhesion physics was FITTED against -- 133 bonds holding 2.0 N on a flat
  ; 26 x 6 cm strip -- and no variant here reproduces that measurement now.
  ; The constants it fitted survive it, in peel/tape.py.
  (:variants handle panel panel_gentle seam decant thread strip wipe)
)
